ing PYBIND11_MODULE\\n- The \\\"run\\\" function must be exposed to Python through the binding\\n- Include proper tensor type conversion between PyTorch tensors and device pointers\\n- Include all necessary PyTorch headers: #include <torch/extension.h>\\n</cpp_file>\\n\\nCode Generation Guidelines:\\n- Use modern HIP features appropriate for MI300X\\n- Optimize memory coalescing and reduce bank conflicts\\n- Utilize shared memory effectively for data reuse\\n- Consider occupancy and register usage\\n- Implement proper error checking with hipGetLastError()\\n- Use appropriate grid and block dimensions for the problem size\\n- Leverage constant memory for frequently accessed read-only data\\n- Use PyTorch tensor API (torch::Tensor) for all tensor arguments in the \\\"run\\\" function\\n- Convert PyTorch tensors to HIP pointers using .data_ptr<float>() or similar methods\\n- Ensure proper HIP stream synchronization and error handling\\n\\n** You MUST use MFMA to utilize the matrix cores on MI300X! ** For each round, you can see your current best solution and the previous round's summary, therefore you can implement the kernel step by step. ROCm/HIP compatibility note (when targeting AMD/MI300X): do not include <ATen/BFloat16.h> (prefer <c10/util/BFloat16.h> or only <torch/extension.h>), avoid HIP/ROCm-only headers/APIs such as <ATen/cuda/...>, <c10/cuda/...>, cudaStream_t/cudaError_t/cudaGetLastError, and output plain source code only (no CDATA, no HTML/XML escaping like &lt; or &gt; inside code).\\n\\nGenerate the updated implementation:\\n\\nWorld Model (persistent across rounds; use it to guide kernel design):\\n{\\n  \\\"computed_signals\\\": {\\n    \\\"round_index\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"trace\\\": {\\n      \\\"latency_ms\\\": null,\\n      \\\"mean_vs_baseline_factor\\\": null,\\n      \\\"reference_latency_ms\\\": null,\\n      \\\"speedup_factor\\\": null,\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"initialized_world_model_no_measurements\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"decision_tree\\